# Pragmatic Flutter Code

Write scalable code that fits the immediate need. Do not over-engineer.

## Solve the current requirement

- Implement only what the requirement needs now
- Do not add unused layers, abstractions, or "just in case" APIs
- Prefer a straightforward class over a generic framework, factory, or extra interface until a second real use case exists
- Scalability means clear structure and easy extension — not premature architecture

## SRP

- One class / function / widget has one clear reason to change
- Split only when responsibilities are already mixed or a second use appears

```dart
// ❌ BAD — fetch, map, and UI in one place
class AnimalsPage extends StatelessWidget {
  Future<void> load() async { /* http + parse + setState */ }
}

 // ✅ GOOD — each unit does one job
class AnimalRepository {
  Future<List<Animal>> fetchAnimals() async { /* http + parse */ }
}

class AnimalListController {
  AnimalListController(this._repository);
  final AnimalRepository _repository;
  List<Animal> animals = [];

  Future<void> load() async {
    animals = await _repository.fetchAnimals();
  }
}
```

## DRY

- Extract shared logic only after duplication is real (same logic in 2+ places)
- Do not create shared helpers for a single call site

## Accurate naming

- Names must describe what the thing is or does (`AnimalListController`, not `Manager` / `Helper` / `Data`)
- Prefer domain words over vague tech words (`fetchAnimals`, not `getData` / `process`)

```dart
// ❌ BAD
class DataHelper {
  Future<dynamic> getStuff() async { ... }
}

// ✅ GOOD
class AnimalRepository {
  Future<List<Animal>> fetchAnimals() async { ... }
}
```
